(Comparison operators compare values and return a Boolean \(True or False\): - Equal to \(==\): True if values are equal \(10 == 10 is) Tj
T* (True\). Notice that '=' assigns while '==' checks equality. - Not equal \(!=\): True if values differ \(10 != 5 is True\). - Greater than \(>\):) Tj
T* (True if left is strictly greater than right \(age = 25; age > 18 is True\). - Less than \(<\): True if left is strictly less than right \(age = 15;) Tj
T* (age < 18 is True\). - Greater than or equal \(>=\): True if left is greater than or equal to right \(age = 18; age >= 18 is True\). - Less) Tj
T* (than or equal \(<=\): True if left is less than or equal to right \(marks = 40; marks <= 40 is True\). Chained Comparisons: Python) Tj
T* (elegantly supports chained comparisons. For example: 18 <= age <= 60 This is evaluated conceptually as: 18 <= age and age) Tj
T* (<= 60 Chaining makes range boundary checks clean, natural, and expressive.) Tj
