(Bitwise operators work directly on the individual bits of integers. They are fundamental in networking, flags, low-level systems,) Tj
T* (cryptography, and performance-sensitive code: Binary Representation: Decimal 10 = 1010 in binary; Decimal 5 = 0101 in binary.) Tj
T* (- Bitwise AND \(&\): Bit is 1 only when both corresponding bits are 1. 1010 & 0101 = 0000 \(0 in decimal\). - Bitwise OR \(|\): Bit is 1 if) Tj
T* (at least one corresponding bit is 1. 1010 | 0101 = 1111 \(15 in decimal\). - Bitwise XOR \(^\): Bit is 1 when corresponding bits are) Tj
T* (different. 1010 ^ 0101 = 1111 \(15 in decimal\). - Bitwise NOT \(~\): Inverts all bits. Follows the integer rule ~x = -\(x + 1\). ~5 = -\(5 +) Tj
T* (1\) = -6. - Left Shift \(<<\): Shifts bits to the left, appending zeros \(multiplies by 2 per position\). 5 << 1 \(0101 -> 1010\) = 10. - Right) Tj
T* (Shift \(>>\): Shifts bits to the right \(divides by 2 per position\). 10 >> 1 \(1010 -> 0101\) = 5.) Tj
