(Scope defines the region of a program where a variable name is recognized and accessible: 1. Local Scope: Variables defined) Tj
T* (inside a function \(including its parameters\) belong to the local scope of that function. They come into existence when the function) Tj
T* (is called and are destroyed when the function finishes. They cannot be accessed from outside the function. 2. Global Scope:) Tj
T* (Variables declared at the top level of a script/module outside of any function belong to the global scope. They can be read from) Tj
T* (anywhere within the module. 3. The global Keyword: If a function needs to reassign or modify a global variable, it must explicitly) Tj
T* (declare it with 'global var_name'. Best Practice: Minimize the use of global variables. Relying on global state makes code brittle,) Tj
T* (hard to test, and difficult to debug. Prefer passing needed data via parameters and returning results.) Tj
