(1. Memory Architecture: Volatile RAM vs. Persistent Storage) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 9 Tf
10.3499999999999996 TL
0.2 0.255 0.333 rg
42.5196850393700814 267.8742519685041543 Td
(To understand file handling, you must first understand the fundamental computer hardware hierarchy: 1. Central Processing Unit) Tj
T* (\(CPU\): The CPU executes instructions at billions of cycles per second. However, it possesses virtually no internal storage) Tj
T* (besides tiny, ultra-fast registers and CPU caches. 2. Main Memory \(RAM - Random Access Memory\): Variables, lists,) Tj
T* (dictionaries, and active objects live inside RAM. While RAM provides lightning-fast nanosecond read/write access, it is strictly) Tj
T* (volatile. The moment your Python script terminates, crashes, or the computer shuts down, all memory addresses allocated to) Tj
T* (your variables are reclaimed by the operating system. 3. Secondary Memory \(SSD / HDD / Cloud Storage\): Secondary storage is) Tj
T* (non-volatile. Files stored on disk retain their exact byte sequences indefinitely. Disk access is slower than RAM \(measured in) Tj
T* (microseconds or milliseconds rather than nanoseconds\), but provides virtually unlimited, inexpensive permanent storage. File) Tj
T* (processing is the deliberate, controlled pipeline of reading byte sequences from persistent secondary memory into temporary) Tj
T* (RAM for computation, and writing computed results back to secondary storage for permanent archival.) Tj
