(At the physical storage level, all files are simply sequences of binary bits \(0s and 1s\). The distinction between file types lies in) Tj
T* (how those bytes are interpreted: 1. Text Files \(.txt, .py, .csv, .json, .log, .md\): A text file is a sequence of characters organized) Tj
T* (into lines. Each character is encoded into bytes using a standardized encoding scheme. In modern computing, UTF-8 \(Unicode) Tj
T* (Transformation Format - 8 bit\) is the global standard, capable of representing every character across English, Greek, Cyrillic,) Tj
T* (Chinese, Arabic, emojis, and mathematical symbols. Always specify encoding='utf-8' when opening text files to prevent) Tj
T* (cross-platform encoding errors \(such as Windows defaulting to cp1252 while Linux defaults to UTF-8\). 2. Binary Files \(.jpg, .png,) Tj
T* (.mp3, .pdf, .zip, .exe\): Binary files store raw non-text byte streams intended for specific software interpreters \(e.g. image) Tj
T* (decoders or audio players\). Opening a binary file in text mode causes decoding exceptions. Binary mode is declared by) Tj
T* (appending 'b' to the mode string \(e.g. 'rb' or 'wb'\). 3. The Newline Character Convention: Lines in text files end with an invisible) Tj
T* (newline marker. Across operating systems: - Linux / macOS: Uses \(Line Feed, ASCII 10\). - Windows: Uses \(Carriage Return +) Tj
T* (Line Feed, ASCII 13 + ASCII 10\). Python's universal newline mode automatically translates OS-specific line breaks into standard) Tj
T* (during text reading.) Tj
