(Writing files in Python requires caution. Understanding mode behavior prevents accidental data loss: 1. The Write Mode \('w'\)) Tj
T* (Truncation Warning: Opening an existing file in 'w' mode instantly wipes out all existing contents on disk before you even issue a) Tj
T* (write command! If you need to keep existing data, never use 'w'. 2. The Append Mode \('a'\): Mode 'a' moves the cursor to the end) Tj
T* (of the file. New write statements append text onto the bottom of the existing content without modifying earlier lines. 3. The) Tj
T* (Exclusive Creation Mode \('x'\): Mode 'x' safely creates a new file. If a file with the given name already exists, Python immediately) Tj
T* (raises FileExistsError instead of overwriting it. This is essential for audit logs, invoices, and transaction ledgers. 4. Manual) Tj
T* (Newline Responsibility: Unlike print\(\), the file.write\(\) method does NOT automatically add a newline character to the end of the) Tj
T* (string. You must explicitly append ' ': fout.write\("First line of record "\) fout.write\("Second line of record "\) 5. Buffer Flushing) Tj
T* (\(flush\(\)\): For performance, Python buffers write operations in RAM before committing them to physical disk. Closing the file) Tj
T* (automatically flushes the buffer. You can force immediate OS synchronization using fout.flush\(\).) Tj
ET
