(Historically, Python programmers relied on the os and os.path modules, which treated paths as awkward raw strings with) Tj
T* (platform differences \(e.g. backslashes '\\' on Windows vs forward slashes '/' on Unix\). Python 3.4+ introduced the pathlib module,) Tj
T* (which treats file system paths as rich, cross-platform objects: from pathlib import Path # Create a path object \(handles) Tj
T* (Windows/POSIX slashes automatically\) data_dir = Path\('data'\) / 'reports' file_path = data_dir / '2026_audit.log' # Path) Tj
T* (introspection: print\(file_path.name\) # '2026_audit.log' print\(file_path.stem\) # '2026_audit' print\(file_path.suffix\) # '.log') Tj
T* (print\(file_path.parent\) # 'data/reports' print\(file_path.exists\(\)\) # True or False print\(file_path.is_file\(\)\) # True or False # Convenient) Tj
T* (reading & writing: file_path.write_text\("Audit record completed.", encoding='utf-8'\) content = file_path.read_text\(encoding='utf-8'\)) Tj
