(1. The Anatomy of Software Defects: Syntax, Runtime & Semantic Errors) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 9 Tf
10.3499999999999996 TL
0.2 0.255 0.333 rg
42.5196850393700814 255.9687401574805108 Td
(Every software bug falls into one of three distinct categories, each requiring different detection and resolution strategies: 1.) Tj
T* (Syntax Errors \(Compile-Time / Parsing Errors\): Occur when source code violates Python's formal grammar rules \(missing) Tj
T* (colons, mismatched parentheses, invalid indentation, unclosed quotes\). - When Detected: Prior to execution, while the Python) Tj
T* (parser is compiling source code into bytecode. - Characteristic: No line of code is executed. Python halts immediately with a) Tj
T* (SyntaxError pointing to the token where parsing failed. 2. Runtime Exceptions \(Execution-Time Errors\): The syntax is) Tj
T* (mathematically valid, but the Python virtual machine encounters an impossible or illegal operation during execution \(e.g. dividing) Tj
T* (by zero, accessing an out-of-bounds list index, opening a missing file, or dereferencing a non-existent dictionary key\). - When) Tj
T* (Detected: During live program execution. - Characteristic: Without protective try/except blocks, Python aborts execution and) Tj
T* (emits a Traceback. 3. Semantic / Logic Errors \(Silent Flaws\): The program compiles perfectly and runs without crashing or) Tj
T* (throwing any exceptions, but produces incorrect outputs or behaves unpredictably \(e.g. calculating gross margin using \(revenue) Tj
T* (+ cost\) instead of \(revenue - cost\), off-by-one loop indexing, or mutating shared mutable default arguments\). - When Detected:) Tj
T* (Through unit testing, code review, or user complaints. - Characteristic: The hardest bugs to diagnose because the Python) Tj
T* (interpreter cannot help you identify them.) Tj
ET
