(2. The Python Exception Hierarchy & The Bare 'except:' Anti-Pattern) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 9 Tf
10.3499999999999996 TL
0.2 0.255 0.333 rg
42.5196850393700814 756.8506299212598378 Td
(All Python exceptions are organized in an object-oriented inheritance tree rooted at BaseException. Understanding this hierarchy) Tj
T* (is paramount for writing safe error-handling code: BaseException --- SystemExit \(Triggered by sys.exit\(\); should NEVER be) Tj
T* (caught in normal code\) --- KeyboardInterrupt \(Triggered when the user hits Ctrl+C to stop a program\) --- GeneratorExit ---) Tj
T* (Exception \(The root class for all regular runtime errors\) --- ArithmeticError \(ZeroDivisionError, OverflowError\) --- LookupError) Tj
T* (\(IndexError, KeyError\) --- ValueError \(Invalid argument value, e.g. int\('hello'\)\) --- TypeError \(Operation on inappropriate data type\)) Tj
T* (--- OSError \(FileNotFoundError, PermissionError, ConnectionError\) --- ... user-defined custom exceptions The Disastrous 'Bare) Tj
T* (except:' Anti-Pattern: Never write: try: process_data\(\) except: # BARE EXCEPT - DANGEROUS! pass Why Bare except: is) Tj
T* (catastrophic: 1. It catches BaseException, meaning if the user presses Ctrl+C to terminate your script, Python traps the) Tj
T* (KeyboardInterrupt and refuses to stop! 2. It swallows typing mistakes \(e.g. NameError if you mistype a variable name as priint) Tj
T* (instead of print\), completely masking defects and creating 'ghost bugs' that take days to isolate. Always catch specific exception) Tj
T* (classes \(e.g. except \(ValueError, KeyError\):\) or at maximum except Exception as e:.) Tj
ET
BT
/F2 11 Tf
12.6499999999999986 TL
0.004 0.2 0.137 rg
42.5196850393700814 614.5514173228345953 Td
(3. Complete Lifecycle of try, except, else & finally) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 9 Tf
10.3499999999999996 TL
0.2 0.255 0.333 rg
42.5196850393700814 600.3781889763779418 Td
(Python provides a four-part control structure for exception management. Each clause serves a strictly defined operational) Tj
T* (purpose: 1. try Block: Encloses only the specific statements that might raise an expected exception. Keep try blocks as compact) Tj
T* (as possible to prevent accidentally masking unrelated errors. 2. except Block\(s\): Executes ONLY if an exception matching the) Tj
T* (declared type occurs inside the try block. Multiple except blocks can be chained to handle different error conditions) Tj
T* (independently. 3. else Block: Executes ONLY if the try block completes successfully with ZERO exceptions raised. - Why use) Tj
T* (else? Placing non-dangerous follow-up code inside else prevents accidentally catching exceptions raised by the follow-up code) Tj
T* (itself, maintaining clean separation of concerns. 4. finally Block: Guaranteed to execute in 100% of execution scenarios,) Tj
T* (regardless of whether: - The try block ran successfully, - An expected exception was handled, - An unhandled exception was) Tj
T* (raised, - Or execution exited early via a return, break, or continue statement! - Primary Use Case: Deterministic cleanup \(closing) Tj
T* (network sockets, releasing database locks, or removing temporary files\).) Tj
ET
BT
/F2 11 Tf
12.6499999999999986 TL
