(In addition to catching errors, robust code deliberately signals invalid states to callers by raising exceptions: 1. The raise) Tj
T* (Statement: Use raise followed by an exception instance: if withdraw_amount > balance: raise ValueError\(f"Insufficient funds:) Tj
T* (requested {withdraw_amount}, available {balance}"\) 2. Creating Custom Exception Classes: In enterprise systems, generic) Tj
T* (exceptions \(like ValueError\) are too ambiguous. Creating custom exception classes allows callers to catch domain-specific) Tj
T* (failures cleanly: class BankingError\(Exception\): """Base exception for all banking operations.""" pass class) Tj
T* (InsufficientFundsError\(BankingError\): """Raised when an account does not have sufficient balance.""" def __init__\(self,) Tj
T* (requested, balance\): super\(\).__init__\(f"Cannot withdraw {requested}; balance is only {balance}"\) self.requested = requested) Tj
T* (self.balance = balance class AccountSuspendedError\(BankingError\): """Raised when attempting transactions on locked) Tj
T* (accounts.""" pass) Tj
