(When an unhandled exception terminates a Python script, it outputs a Traceback. A traceback is a chronological reverse) Tj
T* (snapshot of the call stack: Structure of a Traceback: 1. Header: 'Traceback \(most recent call last\):' Indicates that the oldest call is) Tj
T* (at the top, and the actual point of failure is at the very bottom! 2. Call Stack Frames: Each stack frame lists: - File path: File) Tj
T* ("/var/app/billing.py" - Line number: line 142 - Function or scope: in calculate_tax - Source code snippet: return subtotal *) Tj
T* (tax_rates[country] 3. Final Error Banner: The bottom-most line specifies the exact Exception Class and Error Message: KeyError:) Tj
T* ('CA' How to Read a Traceback Systematically: - Step 1: Look at the BOTTOM line first to identify WHAT went wrong \(Exception) Tj
T* (type + message\). - Step 2: Scan upward to find the LAST file that YOU authored \(ignoring third-party library internals\). - Step 3:) Tj
T* (Jump directly to that file and line number to inspect the offending statement.) Tj
