(Dr. Charles Severance outlines four deliberate, scientific actions to systematically conquer difficult bugs: 1. Reading: Carefully) Tj
T* (read your source code aloud or to a peer. Scrutinize whether the code says what you INTENDED it to say, rather than assuming) Tj
T* (it does. 2. Running: Experiment with small, isolated components. Change variable values, test boundary conditions, or verify) Tj
T* (behavior inside the interactive REPL. 3. Ruminating \(Thinking Deeply\): Step away from the keyboard! Formulate clear, testable) Tj
T* (hypotheses: 'If hypothesis A is true, then variable X should equal 0 at line 45.' Go back to test your hypothesis. 4. Retreating: If) Tj
T* (your debugging attempts have added messy print statements and broken working code further, retreat! Use Git \(git checkout or) Tj
T* (git restore\) to return to your last known working state and approach the problem with a fresh perspective.) Tj
