(Python 3.7 introduced the built-in breakpoint\(\) function, which drops execution into an interactive terminal debugger powered by) Tj
T* (pdb \(Python Debugger\): def compute_payroll\(employees\): for emp in employees: rate = emp['hourly_rate'] hours =) Tj
T* (emp['hours_worked'] breakpoint\(\) # Execution freezes here! Interactive prompt opens. gross = rate * hours ... Essential pdb) Tj
T* (Commands: - n \(next\): Execute the current line and advance to the next line in the current function. - s \(step\): Step into the) Tj
T* (function call on the current line. - c \(continue\): Continue execution until the next breakpoint is encountered or program finishes. -) Tj
T* (p <expression> \(print\): Evaluate and display the value of any variable or expression \(e.g. p emp['hourly_rate']\). - l \(list\): Display) Tj
T* (11 lines of source code surrounding the current execution pointer. - w \(where\): Print the complete stack trace leading to the) Tj
T* (current frame. - q \(quit\): Abort the debugger and terminate script execution immediately.) Tj
