(To appreciate object-oriented programming, consider how procedural code manages state: Procedural Approach: In procedural) Tj
T* (code, data structures and functions exist independently: account_owner = "Ananya" account_balance = 5000.0 def) Tj
T* (deposit\(balance, amount\): return balance + amount def withdraw\(balance, amount\): return balance - amount if balance >=) Tj
T* (amount else balance Weaknesses of Procedural Architecture: 1. State Disconnection: The variables account_owner and) Tj
T* (account_balance have no formal relationship in Python's memory. Any function in the codebase can accidentally overwrite) Tj
T* (account_balance directly. 2. Inability to Scale: Supporting 10,000 distinct accounts requires complex parallel arrays or dictionary) Tj
T* (lists, requiring manual passing of state variables into every function call. 3. Violation of Encapsulation: Business rules \(such as) Tj
T* ('balance cannot be negative'\) cannot be guaranteed because callers can manipulate data variables freely. The Object-Oriented) Tj
T* (Approach: OOP solves this by uniting data and behavior into an indivisible unit: class BankAccount: def __init__\(self, owner,) Tj
T* (balance=0.0\): self.owner = owner self.balance = balance def deposit\(self, amount\): if amount <= 0: raise ValueError\("Deposit) Tj
T* (must be positive"\) self.balance += amount) Tj
