(4. Custom Character Sets \([...]\) & Negated Sets \([^...]\)) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 9 Tf
10.3499999999999996 TL
0.2 0.255 0.333 rg
42.5196850393700814 392.8821259842519567 Td
(Square brackets create custom character sets, matching any single character from the enclosed collection: 1. Explicit Sets:) Tj
T* ([aeiou] -> Matches any single lowercase vowel. [02468] -> Matches any single even digit. 2. Character Ranges \(-\): [a-z] ->) Tj
T* (Matches any single lowercase ASCII character. [A-Z] -> Matches any single uppercase ASCII character. [0-9] -> Matches any) Tj
T* (decimal digit. [a-zA-Z0-9] -> Matches any alphanumeric character. 3. Negated Character Sets \([^...]\): Placing a caret \(^\)) Tj
T* (immediately inside the opening bracket inverts the set: [^0-9] -> Matches any character that is NOT a digit. [^aeiouAEIOU] ->) Tj
T* (Matches any character that is NOT an English vowel. [^s,;] -> Matches any character that is not whitespace, comma, or) Tj
T* (semicolon.) Tj
ET
BT
/F2 11 Tf
12.6499999999999986 TL
0.004 0.2 0.137 rg
42.5196850393700814 298.2049606299212883 Td
(5. Repetition Quantifiers: Greedy vs. Non-Greedy Matching) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 9 Tf
10.3499999999999996 TL
0.2 0.255 0.333 rg
42.5196850393700814 284.0317322834645211 Td
(Quantifiers dictate how many times the preceding character or group may repeat: Quantifier Syntax: - * \(Asterisk\): 0 or more) Tj
T* (times \(optional, repeatable\). - + \(Plus\): 1 or more times \(mandatory at least once\). - ? \(Question Mark\): 0 or 1 time \(optional,) Tj
T* (non-repeatable\). - {n}: Exactly n times \(e.g. d{4} matches a 4-digit year\). - {min,max}: Between min and max times \(e.g. d{2,4}\).) Tj
T* (The Greedy vs. Non-Greedy Trap: By default, Python regex quantifiers \(*, +, {m,n}\) are GREEDY. They consume as many) Tj
T* (characters as possible before allowing the pattern to complete: Sample String: '<p>First paragraph</p><p>Second) Tj
T* (paragraph</p>' Greedy Pattern: r'<p>.*</p>' Result: Matches from the VERY FIRST <p> to the VERY LAST </p>, capturing both) Tj
T* (paragraphs in a single giant string! The Non-Greedy Solution \(?\): Appending ? to any quantifier makes it non-greedy \(lazy\),) Tj
T* (consuming the MINIMUM number of characters necessary to satisfy the match: Non-Greedy Pattern: r'<p>.*?</p>' Result:) Tj
T* (Correctly extracts two independent matches: '<p>First paragraph</p>' and '<p>Second paragraph</p>'.) Tj
ET
BT
/F2 11 Tf
12.6499999999999986 TL
0.004 0.2 0.137 rg
42.5196850393700814 165.5435433070865656 Td
(6. Parentheses for Grouping vs. Extraction \(Capture Groups\)) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 9 Tf
10.3499999999999996 TL
0.2 0.255 0.333 rg
42.5196850393700814 151.3703149606297984 Td
(Parentheses \(\) in regular expressions serve a dual purpose: 1. Grouping: Treating multiple tokens as a single unit for repetition:) Tj
T* (r'\(ab\)+' matches 'ab', 'abab', 'ababab'. 2. Extraction Filtering: When using re.findall\(\), parentheses tell Python: 'Match the entire) Tj
T* (outer pattern, but extract ONLY the substring enclosed inside the parentheses!' Example \(MBOX Email Extraction\): Log Line:) Tj
T* ('From: stephen.marquard@uct.ac.za Sat Jan 5' - Without Parentheses: re.findall\(r'^From: S+@S+', line\) Returns: ['From:) Tj
T* (stephen.marquard@uct.ac.za'] \(Includes the unwanted prefix 'From: '\) - With Capture Group: re.findall\(r'^From: \(S+@S+\)', line\)) Tj
T* (Returns: ['stephen.marquard@uct.ac.za'] \(Matches 'From: ' to anchor the line, but extracts ONLY the email address!\)) Tj
ET
